John Edward: Language skills are crucial to world citizens.
Scotland's renovations continue to display academic excitement, and this has persistently lasted in 2018 with another one-year graduation, which are supported by individual and common success in sports, art, music, and other social measures.
These schools, represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools, have more than 30,000 students throughout Scotland, and are working hard to deliver the very best school experience to both students and parents.
The goal of free schools is to prepare their students for further and higher education, their chosen careers, and their position as world citizens.
As an educational instability that can develop and implement a tailored school curriculum, we see that modern languages are still a popular and sought-after electoral course in the schools.
Nelson Mandela wrote: If you speak to a man in a language he understands when the words of his head.
If you talk to him in his own language, reach the words his heart.
This is a powerful reminder that we cannot adhere only to English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From the graduation results of the year we can see that languages are at the top of the table, with the highest performation rates from health schools.
Approximately 68 percent of students who studied foreign languages received a graduate graduate graduator A.
The substitutes, obtained from the 74 member schools of the Free School Council, showed that 72 percent students received the exemplary A in Mandarin, while 72 percent of those who studied German, 69 percent of those who studied French and 63 percent of those who studied Spanish also received A.
This illustrates that health schools in Scotland support foreign languages as a crucial skill children and adolescents will undoubtedly need in the future.
Linguistics are now valued at the same level as science, technology, engineering, and mathematical curriculum for healthcare and elsewhere.
A survey conducted by the United Nations Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 found that the reasons employers gave up to have difficulty placing an unemployed job, 17% could be attributed to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, linguistic skills become increasingly important to clarify young people for a future career.
As more and more job options require linguistic skills, these skills become crucial in a globalized world.
Having learned a second-language language, these lifelong skills will provide real advantages in the future, no matter what career one chooses.
Communicating directly with people from other countries automatically gives a multilanguage person a competitive advantage.
According to a YouGov survey answered by more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75% were unable to speak a foreign language well enough to have a converse, and the only language a two-digit number of participants (15%) could speak.
That's why investment in language training is now crucial to today's children.
Having more languages, especially those associated with emerging economies, will give children better opportunities to find good jobs.
In Scotland if each school teaches different languages.
Many schools will focus on the more classical modern languages, while others will teach the languages that are considered most valuable to the United Kingdom on the road to 2020, such as Mandarin and Japanese.
Regardless of what concerns your child, there will always be a selection of languages to choose between in health schools, with teachers who are experts in this area.
Skotic health schools aim to create a learning environment that will prepare the children and equip them with the knowledge and abilities they need to succeed, no matter what the future holds.
It cannot be disowned in today's global business environment that language is still crucial to the country's future, and this must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should be used as an inter-national communication capabilities.
Free schools will continue to offer this possibilities, diversity, and award for Scotland’s youths.
Let's get the bee le faire.
