Hrow/AIDS: China reports 14% increase in new cases.
China has reported an increase of as much as 14 percent in the number of people living with HIV and AIDS.
Approximately more than 820,000 people are affected in the country, says health officials.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported only in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases have been transmitted through sex, marking a change from before.
Traditionally, HIV spread rapidly in some parts of China through blood transfusions.
However, the number of people who receive HIV in this way is now closer to zero, said Chinese medical personnel at a conference in the Yunnan province.
However, the annual number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has risen by 100,000.
HIV infection through sex is an acute problem in China's LGBT environment.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is said to be high.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies have estimated that 70–90 percent of men who have sex with men eventually marry women.
A great deal of the disease is caused by insufficient protection in these situations.
Since 2003, China's government has promised public access to HIV medicine as part of an effort to deal with the problem.
