Germany’s new warship delayed once again.
German Navy's most recent frigate should have been completed in 2014 to replace old warships of the Cold War, but it will not occur until the earliest next year due to failing systems and galloperating costs, reported local media.
The evaluation of Riheinland-Pfalz-Defalz led by the new frigates in the Baden-Wuerttemberg class has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the newspaper Die Zeit, citing a military spokesperson.
The navy should have been handed over the navy in 2014, but the major problems of delivery pursued the ambitious project.
The four vessels in the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class navy ordered all the way back in 2007, will replace the old frigates of the Bremen class.
It is agreed that they will be fitted with a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft guns and anti-ship missiles, as well as some stealth technologies, such as reducing radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Other important characteristics include longer maintenance periods; it should be possible to use the latest frigates for up to two years away from their home harbour.
Continuous delays, however, the sophisticated warships they were said to give Germany strength at sea already—will be outdated until they are put into service, reports Die Zeit.
The fateful F125 frigate created headlines last year, when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into operation and returned it to Blohm & Voss in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy has returned a ship to a shipowner after delivery.
Little was known about the causes of the return, but the German media outlined a number of important reminiscilities and hard-defensive flammators that made the warship unusable in a combat mission.
It was particularly important because vessels in the Baden-Wuerttemberg class are to be powered by a crew of about 120 sailors – only half the crew of older frigates in the Bremen class.
It was also revealed that the ship has an immense obsessation, reducing performance and limiting the Navy's ability to make upgrades in the future.
The 7,000-ton heavy "Rheinland-Pafalz" is estimated to be twice as heavy as the same class ships used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to defective hardware, the price tag of the entire project, including the training of the crew has begun to become a problem.
It is claimed to have reached stavastating 3.1 billion from the original 2.2 billion euros.
The problems surrounding the new frigates are particularly important in the light of recent warnings that Germany’s naval forces are shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the German government's Defense Committee, confirmed that the navy actually revolverents extinguished for useful ships.
The official said that the problem has escalated over time because old ships were displaced, but there were no new vessels.
He regrets that none of the frigates of Baden-Wuerttemberg class could be used by the navy.
The National Trust eavesdropping on the secret lives of bats.
Recent research carried out at a landing site in the Scottish Highlands is designed to reveal how bats use the landscape in the pursuit of food.
It is hoped that the findings will shed new light on the behaviour of the unique flying mammals and helping to develop future conservation measures.
The study of scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will follow common bats and dwarf bats, as well as brown and water bat at the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Complearies will be deployed at important locations around the property to trace bat activity throughout the season.
NHS personnel and volunteers will also conduct mobile investigations using handheld detectors.
Expert's sound analyses of all recordings will determine the frequencies of the bats and which species do what.
Thereafter, a habitat map and a report will be prepared to create a detailed landscape picture of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, a conservation advisor for NTS, hopes the results will determine which areas of the habitat are most important to the bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will be included in the decision on what measures should be implemented, such as sprinkled grazing grounds and how best to take care of the forest for bats and other associated species.
The bat population of Scotland and the United Kingdom has declined considerably throughout the 19th century.
They are endangered by the construction and construction work that affect breeding sites and curtains of habitat.
Windmills and illumination may also pose a risk, the same with flies and certain chemically treated building materials as well as attacks from house cats.
In fact, bats aren't blind.
However, because of their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears have more use to their ears than their eyes in capturing prey.
They use sophisticated echolocation to find insects and hindrances along the way.
NTS, responsible for maintaining more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land around the country, take bats very seriously.
They have ten skilled experts who regularly conduct research, mapping of breeding sites, and sometimes rescue work.
The organization has also established Scotland's first and its own flag museum at Threave lands in Dumfries and Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
Property manager David Thompson says the property is the ideal territory for them.
Peculiarities in Threave we have a perfect area for bats say he's epitapelling.
We've got the old buildings, a bunch of old trees and the ideal habitat.
But there's still a lot that we don't know about bats, so the work we're doing here and other real estate will help us to understand more what they're gonna need to thrive.
He emphasizes the importance of mapping the bats before maintaining a property, since unwitting devastation of a single breed can kill up to 1,400 females and young, perhaps eroding an entire local population.
Bats are protected, and it's illegal to kill them, harass or disturb them, or destroy their breeding grounds.
Elisabeth Ferrell (Scottish collaborator for the Bat Conservation Trust) has encouraged the public to help with the work.
She says: We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and for many of the species we simply don't know how the pollutants get along.
