Earthquakes and tsunami in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed in a severe earthquake and tsunami that struck the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, according to officials, and the death toll is expected to rise.
Communication is discontinued, and rescue workers have not been able to receive information from Donggala regency, an area north of Palu, closer to the epicenter of the 7.5 earthquake.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here's some key fragments on Palu and Donggala on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital city of Sulawesi, located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi, with an estimated population of 379,800 in 2017.
The city celebrated its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami occurred.
Donggala (Norwegian: Donggala) is a retrieval that extends more than 200 miles [300 km] along more than 200 miles [300 km] with a coastline in the northwestern part of Sulawesi.
The regency, an administrative region below the provincial level, had an estimated population of 2,299,200 in 2017.
Fisheries and Agriculture are the beams of the central province of Sulawesis's economy, particularly the Donggala coastal region.
It is mainly concentrated in Morowali, on the opposite side of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been affected by tsunamis several times over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's relief administration.
In 1938 a tsunami killed more than 200 people, destroying hundreds of homes in Donggala.
A tsunami also encountered West Donggala in 1996 and killed nine people.
Indonesia lies on the seismic ruins of the Pacific Ocean and is regularly affected by earthquakes.
Here are some of the largest earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in the north of Sumatra on 26 December, sparked a tsunami that hit 14 countries and killed 266,000 people along the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half in Aceh.
2005: A series of major earthquakes struck the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds died on the island of Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A magnitude 6.8 earthquake hit Java, Indonesia’s most populous island, triggering a tsunami attacking the southern coast.
2009: An earthquake of 7.6 occurred near the city of Padang, the capital of West Sumatra Province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A magnitude 7.5 earthquake hit one of the Mentawai Islands, off Sumatra, triggering a tsunami of up to 10 metres (10 ft), which raged dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow earthquake struck Pidie Jaya in Aceh, causing devastation and panic while humans were reminded of the ravages of the fatal earthquake and tsunami in 2004.
No tsunami was triggered this time, but more than 100 were killed by concurrent buildings.
As of 2018: Powerful earthquake afflict Indonesia's tourist island Lombok and kills more than 500 people, mostly on the northern side of the island.
The quake devastated thousands of buildings and caused thousands of tourists to be temporarily stranded.
