How Commerce During the U.S. China Can Benefit Beijing
The opening ointments between the United States and China were deafening, and although the fight is far from over, a long-term rift may be favorable to Beijing.
Donald Trump, U.S. president, fired the first warning earlier this year by placing customs on important Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel, and aluminium.
The most important escalation took place this week with new tariff rates worth $200 billion, which means customs of half of all goods imported to the United States from China.
Of course, Beijing has retaliated each time, at the latest with a five - to 10 - percent on U.S. merchandise worth $60 billion (U.S.).
China has pledged to match the U.S.'s attack, and the world's second-largest economies will probably not raise the white flag immediately.
Having Washington slowing down means succumbing, but public submission to the United States would be overly humbly for the Chinese president, Xi Jinping.
Experts, however, say that if Beijing plays its cards correctly, the pressure around the trade war with the U.S. can provide a long-term positive outlay for China by reducing the interdependence between the two economies.
