Update on algae resurgence: Concentrations are reduced in Pinella, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest report from the Florida Fishing and Wildlife Commission (FWC) shows a general decline in red alga concentrations for parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, stained resurgions are reported in areas of Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte, and Collier County, suggesting reduced concentrations.
A resurgence of the redneck extends about 210 km (210 mi) along the coast, from the north of Pinellas to the south of Lee County.
Spots are discovered about 10 miles (16 km) off the coast of Hillsborough County, but fewer locations than last week.
It has also been observed in Pasco County.
It has been reported that medium-scale concentrations are on or off the coast of Pinellas County in the last week, low to high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, natural to high concentrations in Manatee County, natural to high concentrations on or off the Sarasota County coast.
There's still an irritation in Pinella, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier County.
There was no mention of irritation in the airways in northwestern Florida during the last week.
